References: <nCTphC6Z97I.Ik3mODY2Ll0J@stan-users.groups.google.com> <nCTphC6Z97I.T6UN2SMNNd8J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/nCTphC6Z97I/m/2pVYaTqMvzkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

 "Error in function stan::prob::bernoulli_logit_log(N4stan5agrad3varE): Logit transformed probability parameter[0]  is 1.#QNAN:0, but must not be nan!Rejecting proposed initial value with zero density."

A common reason to have a NaN is that you've forgotten to fill in a parameter somewhere.

In this case, I'm guessing your problem is just a little typo in line 47, which uses c2 instead of c3.  I hope changing lines 46-48 to the following will fix it.

  for (i in 1:c3) {
    alpha3[c3_idx[i]] <- 0;
  }

On Mon, Jun 30, 2014 at 4:54 PM, Rick Farouni <rfar...@gmail.com> wrote:

>
> Thanks Arnold,
>
> I tried to implement your code but for some reason I keep getting an error message "Error in function stan::prob::bernoulli_logit_log(N4stan5agrad3varE): Logit transformed probability parameter[0]  is 1.#QNAN:0, but must not be nan!Rejecting proposed initial value with zero density."
>
> I have tried many things but nothing seems to work. I have attached the code. The indices in the data block are the following
>
> p2 <- 7
> p2_idx <- 1:7
> c2 <- 8
> c2_idx <- 8:15
> p3 <- 8
> p3_idx <- 8:15
> c3 <- 7
> c3_idx <- 1:7
>
>
> Rick,
>
>
>
>
> On Saturday, June 28, 2014 4:11:40 PM UTC-4, Jeffrey Arnold wrote:
> > You're going to have to do something similar. There's no NA in stan or indexing, so you have to come up with ways to make the indexing easy. There are several ways of doing what you want to do. One way of doing what you want is have beta2 and beta3 be transformed parameters of length 7. For beta2 you'd set of the group you'd want to drop to 0.  Here's an example that only uses beta2.
