I think the issue here is appropriately quantifying the uncertainty in predictions rather than "reducing" it. If there is a lot of uncertainty due to measurement error from your instrument, this should be propagated into predictions (assuming that by predicting you mean that you are trying to predict new data points). You could use posterior predictive checks (or better, cross-validation, if possible) to see how well the model actually predicts the data. Then you can also better decide whether you like the poisson or log-normal better (I'm suspecting the log-normal does a better job).

