Maybe I'm missing something, but it seems like this hack would actually change the solution (i.e., it changes the objective function).

That said, there are principled ways to do penalty methods like this, one really popular example is the augmented Lagrangian method however it also suffers from the same difficulties as the log-parameterization: values on or close to a boundary can be hard to reach.  That said, for simple inequality constraints, there are easier and more efficient approaches.

Anyway, until we actually see the data/output from David's model it's impossible to know if this is what's actually going on or not.

Cheers,
Marcus

