> Thanks, I'll keep that in mind! Not sure what the n_divergent statistic is though.

It's discussed in the manual and http://arxiv.org/abs/1312.0906, and is displayed in the bin/print output.

> And yes, importance sampling fails hard in case of highly curved posteriors and unguided samples, but that's actually what incremental mixed importance sampling tries to address. The algorithm iteratively resamples highly curved areas of the posterior at a higher resolution (and reweights those sample appropriately) until some satisfactory number of different samples has been acquired. I haven't tried it yet, but it sounded promising.

None of these adaptive algorithms scale well to high dimensions.  If you're lucky enough to find a point with high curvature, sampling around it in any naive way is even more unlikely to find neighboring points
with high curvature.  High dimensions are hard and importance sampling is never the answer, but I'll leave that for you to find empirically.

