On Monday, July 21, 2014 9:40:47 PM UTC-4, david...@gmail.com wrote:
> I have a question about the viability of using Stan as an implementation of HMC/NUTS for an MCMC kernel - but used in combination with other MCMC kernels that I would like to write myself.

Any kind of an external subroutine would be challenging. For continuous unknowns, at a minimum, you have to get the gradient information from the external subroutine back into the Stan internals, and there is no way to do that except to write custom C++ code.

> There are a few interesting applications such as:
> * a way to handle discrete parameters (i.e. do that bit outside Stan) - although sounds like this will be solved shortly anyway...

Won't be solved shortly, although it is probably the most common request.

> * infinite dimensional models.

There was some thread about Dirichlet Processes recently

> * applying kernels to annealed distributions e.g. annealed importance sampling

Pretty sure this will never be in Stan, but thermodynamic sampling will probably be in Stan 3.x

http://andrewgelman.com/2014/05/20/thermodynamic-monte-carlo/

> * explicitly using tricks like this: http://andrewgelman.com/2011/10/19/an-interweaving-transformation-strategy-for-boosting-mcmc-efficiency/

I don't know about that particular trick but the centered vs. non-centered parameterizations is something people have been thinking about

http://arxiv.org/abs/1312.0906

Ben

