Thanks Ben,

I wrote a slightly more elaborate script which seems to confirm your point...  it seems something is being forgotten or reset in the Markov chain and it doesn't have detailed balance.. (probably as you say the derivatives).  It still puzzles me why this is necessary... (as Stan can compute these - and they cannot be passed to sampling()).

Here I hoped to get around the limitations by making the Stan kernel apply to p(y|mu)  and then sampling mu straight from p(mu) - but it doesn't seem to work (although poor mixing might also be the problem).

I hear what you say about the hard distinction, between data and parameters - but what that means in practice still puzzles me....   The way I see it if everything is collected as needed from sample() and the passed back to it - I don't see why this can't work.  Maybe momentum / derivatives are the key problem... (although I thought momentum was an independent draw)

m <- stan_model(model_code = 'data {real mu;} parameters {real y;} model {y ~ normal(mu,1);}')

innersamp <- 1
numsamples <- 10000
f <- sampling(m, ,data=list('mu'=mu),iter = innersamp,warmup=0,chains=1)
samp <- matrix(0,1,numsamples)

for (ii in 1:numsamples) {
    loglike <- -0.5*(extract(f)$y-mu)^2
    f <- sampling(m, iter = innersamp,warmup=0,chains=1,data=list('mu'=mu),init=list(list('y'=extract(f)$y[innersamp],'lp__'=loglike)))
    mu <- rnorm(1,0,1)
    samp[ii] <- extract(f)$y[innersamp];
}

print(apply(samp,1,sd))# should be root 2  (seems to be lower)
# samp[1:numsamples-1]==samp[2:numsamples] # check accept/reject

