I think there is one hard-coded path in there for loading the file and maybe something else that needs tweeking but if after that you're actually getting errors I'll recheck my files and upload more carefully.

>
> I hadn't thought about using splines, but it is not clear to me if doing so solves everything.  Maybe you can analytically integrate under a non-negative function of them which would change things....

The nice thing about using a gaussian basis for the splines with weights bounded positive is that you can integrate analytically and that's what I did for this code.  That's why the expression for lambda is in terms of a sum of CDF's. I'm pretty sure this is straightforward using any pdf with a nice CDF for a basis.

>
> For this model, the likelihood contains the following terms
>
> 1 ~ poisson(sigmoid(lambda))
> 0 ~ poisson(sigmoid(-latent))
>
> lambda is the size of the data set, where latent is of variable size (which is the big problem for Stan I think).

ok.

>
> The reason for the use of the 1/0 is that for a point process there is a small number of locations where a single event happens and everywhere else nothing happens - you never evaluate the poisson with a count greater than 1.

ok, that's what you'd get for a continuous time survival model too, I don't see why count can't be zero/1...

>
> it looks to me like you are instead modelling
>
> count[ii] ~ poisson(lambda(ii))
>
> which appears at face value to be a different model.  Apologies, in advance if I have misunderstood...  Maybe we should talk more about this out of the thread...  my email is david...@gmail.com.

I don't see why one can't be turned into the other.  I think when you code a continuous time survival model based on a poisson process you end up with something like you are doing but it should
still be fine with modification of the code.  That's on my to-do list so I will probably post both of these to the list to get comments in a separate thread (I guess you're right we're off-topic here)

Krzysztof

