On Thursday, July 24, 2014 1:15:15 PM UTC-4, Yuanchu Dang wrote:
> Thanks, Ben, for your code!
>
> I can see now that doing my sampling with Stan may be tedious... and using only upper or lower bound in the bracket may not be a reproducible way for A with multiple rows. Another concern will be that (in this particular case) it seems that Stan will first sample x1 from [0, 1], then sample x2 from [0, 1 - x2] and then x3 from [0, 1 - x1 - x2]. But mathematically this will produce a different distribution than the uniform triangle of (1, 0, 0), (0, 1, 0) and (0, 0, 1).

Using the notation from

http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_of_linear_equations#Matrix_solution

here is how it could (in principle) be done generically

library(rstan)
library(MASS)

A <- matrix(1, nrow = 1, ncol = 3)
b <- 1
dim(b) <- 1

Ag <- ginv(A)

code <-
  '

data {
  int<lower=1> m;
  int<lower=1> n;
  vector[m] b;
  matrix[m,n] A;

  matrix[n,m] Ag;
}
transformed data {
  matrix[n,n] ImAgA;
  vector[n] Agb;
  Agb <- Ag * b;
  ImAgA <- -Ag*A;
  for(i in 1:n) ImAgA[i,i] <- ImAgA[i,i] + 1.0;
}
parameters {
  vector[n] w;

}
transformed parameters {
  vector<lower=0>[n] x;

  x <- Agb + ImAgA * w;
}
model {

}
'

d <- list(m = 1, n = 3, b = b, A = A, Ag = Ag)
fit <- stan(model_code = code, data = d, init="0")
pairs(fit)

But, I always end up with [edit: no I don't; see next post]

Invalid value of x: Error in function validate transformed params N4stan5agrad3varE: x[1]  is [negative], but must be greater than or equal to 0
If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,
but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.
Error : Posterior is improper. Please check your model.
error occurred during calling the sampler; sampling not done

So, at a minimum, I think you would have to tweak the NUTS settings and even then I doubt it would work well.

Ben

