> it seems the only way to get it into CRAN, which should hopefully simplify
> some of the installs).

In this case, I second CRAN's attitude : this standard greatly simplifies the life of people needing to use R for other purposes than Stan's frontend.

A practicing biostatiscian  has to solves problems (legitimate or not) not yet in Stan's reach. Examples : propensity score matching for observational cohorts comparisons (I hear that J. Hill is on this case, but I found nothing practically useful a few weeks ago), producing p-values to appease an (illiterate) reviewer's  fear (illegitiate but frequent).

To solve these problems, an armada of tools exists, whose use is the bread-and-butter of biostatistics. R is the "greatest common denominator" of these tools. CRAN's standard ensure ome form of consistency between these tools, thus allowing their use in the same installation : one does not have one installation for descriptive statistics, one installation for nonlinear (frequentist) regression, one for Bayesian modeling, etc...

>
> > use a simpler install command (see above) AND GET RID OF THE DAMN INSTALL SCRIPT ?
> >
> > Just my two cents... What do you think ?
>
> For one, Andrew would kill us if we got rid of the install script.

You might shoot back ;-)

> That's because it's cleaned up most of the install procedures for
> "standard" installations, which are very fiddly and error prone.

He's right : a standardized procedure is important. But anything that needs to be done to install Stan properly should be done *from the package* and should use only the tools CRAN standardized on (modulo possible dependencies on other CRAN packages for specific tasks).

If a needed tool is missing from CRAN collection,, the only logical thing to do is ... to package it for the CRAN collection 8--)

>
> We figured people running non-standard installs would be able to
> deal with a little more customization on their own.
>
> Having said that, I have no idea what Sage is (other than what
> I see on their home page: http://www.sagemath.org).

You've read their blurb. Itn fact is a great heap of mathematically-oriented software (with an accent on algebra and algebra-related subjects), but with very useful links to various software such as symbolic computation (Maxima, but also sympy and others, Mathematica if available), numerical computation (octave or Matlab if available), statistics (R ;-), graphics, etc ... and a way to use them from a common "place". It is an extremely enticing set of tools when you happen to have the use of at least two of these tools (in my case, R and Maxima+/-sumpy/scipy +/- octave) : Sage allows some interoperability between these tools (for some values of "some":-) ad a way to interact easily with \LaTeX (much more important than it seems ! I consider siome form of "literate programming" a basic method).

>  Maybe we just need to
> build Stan into Sage so nobody has to install it manually.

R has part of Sage for several years. Since most of the Sage "active team" is mor pure math otriented, R versions tend to be lag, sometimes quite a bit. So it depends on lesser minds with time for more mundane tasks to get updated versions of R on a regular basis. That's what yours truly is doing since about 1 year...

>  Isn't that
> the point of these mega-integrations?

It's a bit more (and a bit less) than that.

> Having said that, we can add special instructions for special versions
> of R.

I'd rather have a strong standard package that does not depend on special care and feeding to get installed.

HTH,

